adjudication.rationale:
In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook authentication uses a direct string comparison:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This is not a constant-time comparison and can, in principle, leak information via timing differences when an attacker can make many requests and measure response times. A constant-time comparison (e.g., `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on Buffers of equal length, or verifying an HMAC) is the standard mitigation for secret comparisons like this.

In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the secret check is:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403)
}
```

If `CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is unset, `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is `undefined`. If the request also omits the header, `req.headers[... ]` will also be `undefined`, making the comparison `undefined !== undefined` false, so the request is treated as authenticated and proceeds to update/create credentials. There is no separate guard rejecting missing secrets, so unauthenticated writes are possible when credential sharing is enabled but the secret is not configured.
